  [codex] ### Executive Judgment
  [codex] The first-eye system is in the wrong lane on these target days. The core problem is structural and interpretive burden misassignment, compounded by exposition template lock-in and one explicit genre misapplication. Days 3-6 flatten distinct Sinai movements into two generic lanes, and day 8 wrongly treats commandment material as narrative while carrying the same obedience frame across the week boundary. This is not a wording problem. The repair is to reassign burdens by scene and covenant movement, then lightly rewrite exposition, action, and prayer around those new anchors.
  [codex] 
  [codex] ### Failure Map
  [codex] - Day/Cluster: `3-4`
  [codex] - Passage: `Exodus 19:9-17`
  [codex] - Failure type: `adjacent-day sameness; burden misassigned; exposition template lock-in`
  [codex] - Primary cause: `mixed` (`structural` and `interpretive`)
  [codex] - What the passage is doing: `19:9-12` is about the Lord establishing mediated hearing and protective boundaries so the people learn to trust the Lord's speaking through Moses. `19:13-17` shifts to consecrated approach and trembling preparation as the people are brought out to meet God at the mountain.
  [codex] - Warranted devotional burden: Day 3 should stress covenant mediation, hearing, and the seriousness of drawing near on God's terms. Day 4 should stress consecrated approach to holy presence and the sobering privilege of meeting God.
  [codex] - Unwarranted drift: `truthfulness under pressure` is not the passage's burden on either day. Pilate/crowd pressure language is imported from another lane and obscures Sinai preparation.
  [codex] 
  [codex] - Day/Cluster: `5-6`
  [codex] - Passage: `Exodus 19:18-25`
  [codex] - Failure type: `adjacent-day sameness; burden misassigned; scene-summary/theological-lane conflict`
